"""Form Three Economics Term One, rows 1–14, including guided practicals 5, 6 and 8."""
from codex_support import *
from lessonlib import num, worked
F = fact

def unit(n,title,note,case,method,facts,**kw):
    concept_lesson("economics","n3-",n,title,note,case,method,facts,"foundations",**kw)

unit(1,"Fundamentals: scarcity, wants and choice",
 "Economics examines choices about scarce resources and the production, distribution and use of goods and services. A want is a desire for something; resources are means used to satisfy wants. Scarcity means resources are limited relative to the wants they could satisfy. It affects rich as well as poor people. Because not every want can be met at once, people rank alternatives and choose. A school, household or government also faces choices about time, land, labour and materials.",
 "Bih has one free afternoon. She wants to revise, help at a stall and visit a friend. Even if no money changes hands, time is scarce. A school in Buea may have enough land for a garden or an extra play area but not both on the same plot. Economics asks how such choices are made and what must be given up.",
 "Identify the decision maker, the resource constraint and the alternatives. Do not define scarcity simply as having no money: a well-funded club may still lack time or space. Explain the connection: limited resources in relation to wants make choice necessary.",[
 F("want","a desire for a good, service or activity","Ayuk would like a bicycle for travel.","Every want is automatically satisfied.","A desire is a want; satisfying it depends on available resources."),
 F("resource","a means that can help satisfy wants","A school uses land, teachers' time and books.","Only cash can be a resource.","Time, land and labour can be resources as well as money."),
 F("scarcity","limited resources relative to competing wants","A club has time for either a debate or a match this afternoon.","Scarcity affects only people with no income.","All decision makers can face limits relative to their wants."),
 F("choice","selection between alternatives","Bih decides to revise rather than visit a friend.","Choice is unnecessary when resources are scarce.","Scarcity makes selection necessary because all wants cannot be met together.")])

unit(2,"Fundamentals: scale of preference and opportunity cost",
 "A scale of preference ranks wants in order of importance to a decision maker. Choice depends on this ranking and on available resources. Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative forgone, not all rejected alternatives added together. It can involve time or an activity rather than money. A monetary cost is an amount paid; it need not measure the full opportunity cost. State what was actually given up and what the decision maker would otherwise have chosen.",
 "Ngozi ranks repairing her school bag above buying a novel, and the novel above buying a bracelet. If she can afford only one, choosing the repair gives up the novel as the next-best alternative. The bracelet is also not bought, but it is not added to the opportunity cost. If she later receives more resources, the feasible set can change without changing the meaning of opportunity cost.",
 "List feasible alternatives, rank them and identify the best rejected option. Do not confuse the chosen benefit with what is forgone. If an exercise asks for a remaining balance, subtract spending from the available budget and keep CFA francs in the answer.",[
 F("scale of preference","a ranking of wants in order of importance","Bih places school-bag repair above a novel and a bracelet.","A preference ranking lists all items as equally important.","A scale of preference shows priority, rather than an unranked list."),
 F("opportunity cost","the next-best alternative forgone by a choice","Bih chooses bag repair; the novel was her best rejected option.","Opportunity cost is the sum of every rejected option.","Use the best forgone alternative, not the total of all alternatives."),
 F("monetary cost","the amount paid for a purchase","A trader pays for a notebook.","The chosen notebook itself is the money paid.","Distinguish the item obtained from the payment made."),
 F("budget constraint","the resource limit on affordable choices","Ayuk cannot buy a basket costing more than the money he has.","A high preference makes any item affordable.","Preference does not remove the resource constraint.")],replacement={3:num("n3-2-balance",2,"Ngozi has {budget} CFA francs and pays {cost} CFA francs to repair a bag. How much remains?","budget-cost",["Remaining budget = {budget} − {cost} = {answer} CFA francs."],vars={"a":{"int":[5,9]},"b":{"int":[1,4]},"budget":{"expr":"a*500"},"cost":{"expr":"b*500"}},misc=[("add","budget+cost","Spending reduces the balance; you added the payment instead of subtracting it."),("spent","cost","That is the amount spent, not the amount remaining.")],where=["budget-cost != cost"],sources=["foundations"],unit="CFA francs")})

unit(3,"Tools of analysis: variables, tables and relationships",
 "Economists organise information using words, tables, diagrams and simple calculations. A variable can take different values, such as quantity sold. A table places observations in labelled rows and columns. Units identify what the values measure. A relationship describes how variables are associated. Association alone does not prove that one variable caused another to change: other influences may matter. Read the title, labels and units before using any values.",
 "A Bamenda trader records baskets of tomatoes sold on market days. A column headed baskets measures quantity, not CFA francs. A second column may record price per basket. Clear labels prevent a learner from adding a price to a quantity as though they measured the same thing. A record of higher sales on sunny days suggests a relationship to investigate, not proof that sunshine alone caused sales.",
 "Name the variable and its unit. Read across the same row when comparing an observation. Keep price per item separate from total expenditure. When drawing a conclusion, say exactly which relationship the data show and which possible causes have not been checked.",[
 F("variable","a quantity or characteristic that can take different values","The number of baskets sold changes from market day to market day.","A variable must have the same value in every observation.","It is called a variable because different values are possible."),
 F("table","information arranged in labelled rows and columns","A trader records each market day beside its quantity sold.","An unlabelled list always identifies the meaning of its values.","Rows, columns and headings are needed to make a table interpretable."),
 F("unit","the measure in which a value is expressed","A heading says the price is in CFA francs per basket.","A price per basket is measured in baskets alone.","CFA francs per basket measures a unit price, not a physical quantity."),
 F("association","an observed relationship between variables","Sunny market days in a small record also show higher sales.","Every association proves a direct cause.","Other factors may explain a relationship; association alone does not establish causation.")])

unit(4,"Tools of analysis: axes, graphs and scales",
 "A graph represents relationships visually. Axes must be labelled with variables and units. A scale states the value represented by equal spaces. Points show paired observations. In a conventional demand diagram, quantity is on the horizontal axis and price on the vertical axis; the convention should be stated rather than guessed. A change in a graph can be misread if an axis is truncated or intervals are unequal. Use labels before interpreting height or slope.",
 "Ngozi compares weekly basket sales with a bar chart. The horizontal axis identifies the weeks and the vertical axis counts baskets. If one marked interval represents several baskets, the height must be read using that scale. A taller bar is not automatically more revenue: revenue also depends on the price charged.",
 "Read the title, both axis labels and the scale. Follow a point or bar to the correct axis, then include the unit. Do not treat a category label as a numerical measurement. If a graph and its table disagree, identify which plotted point or label needs checking.",[
 F("horizontal axis","the axis running across a graph","Quantity labels are placed along the bottom of a demand diagram.","The horizontal axis is always the upright line.","Horizontal runs across; vertical runs up and down."),
 F("vertical axis","the upright axis of a graph","Price is labelled on the upright axis in a conventional demand diagram.","A demand diagram convention puts price on the bottom axis.","The usual school demand convention places price vertically."),
 F("scale","the values assigned to equally spaced marks","A graph key explains how many baskets each interval represents.","Equal spaces can be given arbitrary unequal values without warning.","A linear scale uses consistent numerical intervals for equal spaces."),
 F("plotted point","a position representing a pair of values","A learner marks the position for a stated price and quantity.","A point on a relationship graph represents no values.","A point is interpreted through the values on its axes.")])

def chart_question(n,index=0):
    return num(f"n3-{n}-chart-{index}",2,"Read the bar chart. How many baskets were sold by {labels["+str(index)+"]}?",f"values[{index}]",
      ["Find {labels["+str(index)+"]} on the horizontal axis.","Read its height on the baskets scale: {answer} baskets."],
      vars={"labels":["Bih","Ayuk","Ngozi"],"a":{"int":[2,8]},"b":{"int":[2,8]},"c":{"int":[2,8]},"values":{"expr":"[a,b,c]"}},
      figure="barChartFig(labels,values)",unit="baskets",sources=["foundations"])

unit(5,"Practical preparation: reading a bar chart",
 "A bar chart compares separate categories using bars of equal width and a common scale. Leave gaps between categories. The height measures the value, not the width or colour. Start a simple frequency bar chart at zero to make length comparisons fair. Always label the measured unit. In this guided practical, use the chart on screen, read the values and check them against a table in your exercise book.",
 "The chart compares basket sales by Bih, Ayuk and Ngozi. The names are categories; the vertical values count baskets. Read each bar from the same baseline. Record name and quantity in a small table, then compare the largest and smallest values. These are invented classroom data, not a survey of actual traders.",
 "Write a title, category labels and the baskets unit. Plot equal-width bars at a consistent scale. To check a drawing, reread each height and compare it with the original data. A taller bar represents more baskets in this chart, not automatically a higher price or profit.",[
 F("category","a separate group being compared","The chart has separate bars for named traders.","The width of a category bar is the trader's profit.","Categories identify groups; heights show the measured quantity."),
 F("bar height","the value read against the common scale","Bih's bar reaches a marked baskets value.","The colour alone gives the numerical value.","Read the height on the labelled scale rather than guessing from colour."),
 F("baseline","the common starting line for the bars","Each bar starts at the same zero line.","Each bar can start at a different value without affecting comparison.","Different baselines distort direct comparisons of bar length."),
 F("frequency","the number of occurrences or items counted","A table counts baskets sold by each trader.","A frequency count is automatically a price.","Frequency counts items; prices require a monetary unit.")],replacement={2:chart_question(5),3:chart_question(5,1)},learn_figure="barChartFig(['Bih','Ayuk','Ngozi'],[4,6,3])")

unit(6,"Practical preparation: pie charts and totals",
 "A pie chart divides a whole into sectors. Each sector represents a share of the same total; sector angles add to a full turn. The larger the share, the larger its angle. Calculate a share by dividing the category's value by the total, then multiply by the angle of a full turn. The chart is inappropriate when the categories overlap and cannot form one whole. Keep the total and unit visible.",
 "A classroom record divides a fruit stall's basket sales into oranges, bananas and plantains. These categories form one counted total. The pie chart represents proportions, not the number of traders. A half share occupies half the circle. Before drawing, add all category counts and check that no basket has been counted twice.",
 "Add the values, calculate each fraction and convert it to an angle. Check that the angles sum to {360} degrees. Use a key to identify sectors. Do not use each separate category as its own denominator: every share refers to the common total.",[
 F("sector","a wedge representing part of a whole","The banana share occupies one labelled wedge.","Every sector represents the entire whole.","A sector is part of the pie; all sectors together represent the whole."),
 F("total","the sum of all category values in the chart","A learner adds orange, banana and plantain basket counts.","The total is only the largest category.","Add all categories to find the common denominator."),
 F("share","a category's value divided by the common total","Banana baskets are compared with all fruit baskets counted.","Each category uses a different total chosen to make its share bigger.","Shares of one pie use the same whole."),
 F("angle","the share multiplied by the degrees in a full turn","A half share occupies half the pie.","A larger share must have a smaller sector angle.","Sector angle increases with the share of the same total.")],replacement={3:num("n3-6-angle",2,"A fruit record contains {a} banana baskets out of {a+b+c} baskets altogether. What is the banana sector angle?","a/(a+b+c)*360",["Share = {a} ÷ {a+b+c}.","Angle = {a} ÷ {a+b+c} × {360} = {answer}°."],vars={"a":{"int":[1,3]},"b":{"expr":"4-a"},"c":2},unit="degrees",sources=["foundations"],misc=[("count","a","You gave a basket count; convert the share to degrees."),("full","360","The full turn is for all baskets, not bananas alone.")])},learn_figure="pieFig(['oranges','bananas','plantains'],[2,3,1])")

unit(7,"Utility: total and marginal satisfaction",
 "Utility is the satisfaction a consumer obtains from consuming a good or service. It is subjective: people can value the same item differently. Total utility is satisfaction from all units consumed in a stated situation. Marginal utility is the change in total utility from an additional unit. Diminishing marginal utility means successive units add less satisfaction, other conditions held constant. Falling marginal utility need not mean falling total utility while each additional gain remains positive.",
 "After walking in warm weather, Ayuk may value the first cup of water highly. A later cup can add less satisfaction. In a classroom numerical illustration, utility units are assumed, not scientifically measured happiness. They should not be used to claim that one person's satisfaction is objectively comparable with another's.",
 "Keep total and additional satisfaction separate. Subtract consecutive total-utility values to calculate marginal utility for that increment. Check whether the increment is positive, zero or negative before stating how total utility changes. Hold the consumer and circumstances fixed when discussing diminishing marginal utility.",[
 F("utility","satisfaction obtained from consuming a good or service","Bih enjoys reading a borrowed novel.","Utility always means the money price of a good.","Price is a payment; utility is satisfaction."),
 F("total utility","satisfaction from all units consumed in a stated situation","A record describes Ayuk's satisfaction from all cups so far.","Total utility refers only to the last additional cup.","The last increment is marginal utility, not total utility."),
 F("marginal utility","the change in total satisfaction from an additional unit","A learner compares satisfaction before and after one more cup.","Marginal utility is the total from every cup added together.","Marginal utility measures the increment from the extra unit."),
 F("diminishing marginal utility","successive additional units adding less satisfaction with other conditions fixed","Ayuk's later cups each add less satisfaction than the earlier cup.","Falling marginal utility always makes total utility fall.","Positive but smaller gains still increase total utility.")],replacement={3:num("n3-7-mu",2,"In an assumed classroom utility table, total utility rises from {before} to {before+gain} units after one extra item. Find marginal utility.","gain",["Marginal utility = new total − previous total.","{before+gain} − {before} = {answer} assumed utility units."],vars={"before":{"int":[10,40]},"gain":{"int":[1,9]}},unit="assumed utility units",sources=["foundations"],misc=[("total","before+gain","You reported the new total rather than the extra satisfaction."),("add","before+before+gain","Subtract the previous total; adding totals counts earlier satisfaction again.")])})

unit(8,"Practical preparation: a utility table",
 "A utility table can illustrate the relationship between total and marginal utility for one consumer in fixed circumstances. The figures are hypothetical. Read units in order. For an extra unit, subtract the previous total utility from the new total. Total utility rises when marginal utility is positive, stays unchanged when it is zero and falls when it is negative. A table can illustrate diminishing marginal utility without claiming that satisfaction is directly measurable in everyday life.",
 "Ngozi records an imagined example for portions of a snack. Each additional portion may add less satisfaction. Her exercise book needs separate columns for number consumed, total utility and marginal utility. Using one person's assumed values avoids pretending to compare different people's satisfaction. The practical asks you to calculate and interpret the differences, not to eat until uncomfortable.",
 "Copy the hypothetical totals, compute consecutive differences and label the columns. When an increase becomes smaller, say marginal utility is diminishing; do not say total utility has fallen unless the difference is negative. Check each row against the preceding row rather than against zero.",[
 F("positive marginal utility","an extra unit increasing total utility","A hypothetical total rises after another portion.","A positive increment lowers the total.","A positive change adds to total utility."),
 F("zero marginal utility","an extra unit leaving total utility unchanged","A hypothetical total stays the same after another portion.","Zero additional satisfaction erases all previous satisfaction.","The total remains, but this extra unit adds nothing."),
 F("negative marginal utility","an extra unit reducing total utility","A hypothetical total decreases after another portion.","A negative increment must increase the total.","A negative change reduces the total."),
 F("hypothetical utility table","assumed values used to illustrate a relationship","A teacher invents totals for practice and labels them assumed utility units.","Classroom utility figures prove exact measurements of everyone's happiness.","The figures are an illustration, not objective interpersonal measurements.")],prerequisites=[7],replacement={3:num("n3-8-difference",2,"An assumed utility total changes from {base} to {base+change} after an additional portion. Find marginal utility, including its sign.","change",["New total minus previous total = {base+change} − {base} = {answer}.","A negative answer means the extra portion reduced total utility."],vars={"base":{"int":[20,40]},"change":{"pick":[-5,-2,0,3,6]}},signed=True,unit="assumed utility units",sources=["foundations"],misc=[("reverse","-change","You reversed the subtraction; use new total minus previous total.","change != 0")])})

unit(9,"Economic systems: the basic allocation questions",
 "An economic system organises how resources are owned, production is coordinated and output is distributed. Every society faces what to produce, how to produce and for whom to produce. What concerns the goods and services chosen. How concerns methods and resource combinations. For whom concerns who receives output and on what basis. These questions arise because resources are scarce. A system's label alone does not tell us whether every outcome is fair or efficient.",
 "A community near Buea may decide whether land should support food crops or housing. That is a what question. Choosing hand tools or machinery concerns how. Deciding who receives housing or can buy food concerns for whom. A single project can involve all these questions, but they remain analytically different.",
 "Identify what decision is being made before naming the question. Look for product, method or recipient. Do not turn a question about production technique into one about ownership without evidence. Systems can combine different ways of answering the allocation questions.",[
 F("what to produce","which goods and services to make","A community chooses between a clinic building and a market shelter.","Choosing the product is only a question about the buyer's identity.","What concerns the output selected, not who eventually receives it."),
 F("how to produce","which techniques and resource combinations to use","A farmer chooses between hand tools and a hired machine.","A choice of technique answers only what product to make.","The product may stay fixed while the production method changes."),
 F("for whom to produce","who receives output and on what basis","A housing scheme decides which households qualify.","Distribution is identical to choosing a machine.","Recipients concern distribution; machinery concerns technique."),
 F("allocation","assigning scarce resources among competing uses","A village divides available land between housing and farming.","Allocation is unnecessary when land has competing uses.","Competing uses are precisely why allocation decisions are needed.")])

unit(10,"Economic systems: ownership and coordination",
 "Systems differ in who owns productive resources and how decisions are coordinated. Private ownership places assets under individuals or firms. Public ownership places assets under the state or a public body. Markets coordinate many decisions through exchange and prices; planning coordinates through deliberate decisions and instructions. Traditional arrangements may rely on custom. Actual economies combine institutions, so a single private shop or state project cannot by itself classify an entire national economy.",
 "A family stall in Bamenda may be privately owned, while a public service belongs to a state body. A school can plan its timetable even within a wider market economy. Distinguish the institution being described from the whole country's system. Classification should use ownership and coordination together, rather than whether money appears in the story.",
 "Ask who controls the resource, who makes the decision and what coordinates other decisions. Do not assume public ownership means nobody is responsible, or private ownership means no rules apply. Use mixed arrangements when the evidence shows more than one mechanism.",[
 F("private ownership","productive assets controlled by private individuals or firms","A family owns and operates its stall.","A private stall must be owned by the state.","Private ownership distinguishes the family's control from public ownership."),
 F("public ownership","productive assets owned by the state or a public body","A public body owns a service's equipment.","Public ownership means the equipment belongs to no institution.","A public owner remains an identifiable body with responsibilities."),
 F("market coordination","decisions linked through exchange and prices","Buyers and sellers adjust purchases and sales through prices.","A price mechanism means all decisions are issued as central instructions.","Market exchange is different from central allocation instructions."),
 F("planning","deliberate coordination through decisions and instructions","A body allocates inputs according to a production plan.","Planning cannot occur anywhere within a market economy.","Firms and public bodies can plan even when wider coordination uses markets.")],prerequisites=[9])

unit(11,"Market system: price signals and enterprise",
 "A market system relies substantially on private decisions and exchange to allocate resources. Prices communicate information about willingness to buy and the conditions of supply. Firms respond to expected costs and revenue, while consumers choose within their budgets. Profit is revenue minus cost, not revenue alone. Competition can encourage attention to price and quality, but markets operate within institutions and rules. A market system does not mean that every seller succeeds or that every person can afford every good.",
 "At a Douala market, buyers can compare sellers and decide whether a basket is worth its price. A trader considers how much the stock costs and what buyers will pay. A higher selling price is not automatically a higher profit if costs also rise. This classroom example explains an incentive; it is not advice about how to invest money.",
 "Separate the consumer's choice from the producer's decision. Identify revenue and cost before calculating profit. Look for alternatives when discussing competition. Do not treat an expectation of profit as a guarantee that the business will earn it.",[
 F("price signal","information conveyed through a market price","A trader notices buyers switching away from a more costly basket.","A price signal is a central command fixing every transaction.","Prices influence decentralised choices rather than necessarily issuing a command."),
 F("enterprise","organising resources and bearing business risk","A trader arranges stock, transport and selling space.","Enterprise guarantees a profit on every purchase.","Organising a business involves uncertainty; success is not guaranteed."),
 F("profit","revenue minus the costs counted in the exercise","A classroom business subtracts its stated costs from sales revenue.","Profit is all money received without deducting costs.","Sales receipts are revenue; deduct the costs to find profit."),
 F("competition","rivalry among alternative suppliers or buyers","Customers compare several stalls offering similar goods.","Competition requires there to be only one possible seller.","Alternatives make rivalry possible; one seller is not the defining case.")],replacement={3:num("n3-11-profit",2,"A classroom stall receives {rev} CFA francs in sales and has stated costs of {cost} CFA francs. Find its profit.","rev-cost",["Profit = sales revenue − stated costs.","{rev} − {cost} = {answer} CFA francs."],vars={"a":{"int":[10,20]},"b":{"int":[4,9]},"rev":{"expr":"a*1000"},"cost":{"expr":"b*1000"}},unit="CFA francs",sources=["foundations"],misc=[("revenue","rev","That is revenue; profit deducts costs."),("add","rev+cost","Costs are subtracted from revenue, not added.")])})

unit(12,"Market system: strengths and limits",
 "Market incentives can encourage innovation and responsiveness, but market outcomes also have limits. An externality affects people outside the transaction. A public good is non-rival and non-excludable in the technical sense, not simply anything government owns. Unequal purchasing power can make access unequal. Monopoly power may weaken competition. Assess a market outcome by the evidence and the criterion being used, rather than assuming that private exchange always produces the best social result.",
 "Smoke from a workshop may affect neighbouring households that did not buy its product. A buyer and seller may not account for that effect in their transaction. Another example is a shared warning signal: excluding non-payers can be difficult, and one person's hearing it need not prevent others hearing it. These cases explain why a price alone may not capture every social cost or benefit.",
 "Identify who is affected and whether they are party to the exchange. For a public-good claim, test both rivalry and exclusion. Do not call every publicly provided service a pure public good. Distinguish efficient production from fair access: they are different criteria.",[
 F("externality","an effect on a third party not fully reflected in the transaction","Workshop smoke affects neighbouring residents.","Only the buyer and seller can ever be affected by production.","Effects on neighbours are precisely the third-party issue."),
 F("public good","a good that is non-rival and non-excludable","A common warning signal can alert many people without one person's hearing excluding others.","Every government-owned item is automatically a pure public good.","Ownership is not the test; non-rivalry and non-excludability are."),
 F("market power","ability to influence market conditions rather than merely accept them","A dominant supplier has few rivals and can influence its price.","Having few alternatives guarantees vigorous competition.","Weak alternatives can strengthen a dominant supplier's influence."),
 F("inequality of access","differences in ability to obtain goods or services","Households with different budgets cannot all buy the same basket.","Willingness to buy always means ability to pay.","A desire to buy and the purchasing power to buy are distinct.")],prerequisites=[11])

unit(13,"Planned economic system",
 "A planned system relies substantially on central decisions about resource allocation and production. Planners may set targets, direct inputs and decide priorities. Public ownership is often important in the textbook model. Planning can coordinate large projects or priorities, but requires information about needs, resources and trade-offs. Weak information or incentives can lead to mismatches between targets and actual needs. Discuss the model without assuming that every public project makes an economy fully planned.",
 "An imagined planning office allocates equipment to food production and housing under a common plan. It must decide what to make, how to use resources and who receives output. If information about local needs is poor, the plan can produce too much of one item and too little of another. This is a hypothetical model, not a claim about a particular government's current arrangements.",
 "Identify the decision maker and the instructions given. Compare intended priorities with the information needed to meet them. Consider both coordination benefits and the risks of rigid targets. Avoid the claim that money, choice or local planning must vanish in every economy using planning.",[
 F("central planning","allocation coordinated substantially through central decisions","An imagined office directs inputs to stated production targets.","Central planning means every producer independently follows only market prices.","Central instructions are different from predominantly decentralised price coordination."),
 F("production target","an intended output level or goal in a plan","A planning body sets the amount a workshop should produce.","A target is proof that the output was achieved.","An intended goal and the actual result must be distinguished."),
 F("information problem","difficulty obtaining the knowledge needed for allocation","An office lacks reliable reports of local demand.","A central office automatically knows every local need.","Reliable and timely local knowledge is needed; it is not automatic."),
 F("coordination","aligning related activities toward a goal","A plan links transport, materials and construction schedules.","Coordination eliminates every possible trade-off.","Activities can be aligned while scarce resources still require choices.")])

unit(14,"Mixed economic system",
 "A mixed economy combines market exchange with public action and different ownership arrangements. Private firms and households make many decisions, while public bodies may provide services, regulate activities or pursue distributional aims. The balance varies across countries and sectors. Mixed does not mean an exact equal split. Regulation is not the same thing as ownership: a private firm can be regulated without becoming publicly owned. Evaluate a particular policy through its purpose, mechanism, costs and effects.",
 "A family stall can trade in a market while a public body maintains a road used by buyers and sellers. Rules about weights and product information can shape private exchange. The presence of both activities illustrates a mixed arrangement; it does not supply a numerical share for the whole economy or prove that every intervention works well.",
 "For each example, ask who owns the resource, who makes decisions and what public action is involved. Do not equate mixed with random or unplanned. Compare a policy's intended effect with evidence about its actual effect, including trade-offs and unintended consequences.",[
 F("mixed economy","a combination of market coordination and public action","Private stalls trade alongside publicly provided infrastructure.","A mixed economy must divide all activity into exactly equal halves.","The balance varies; equal shares are not part of the definition."),
 F("regulation","rules shaping how an activity is carried out","Rules require traders to use accurate weights.","Regulating a firm necessarily transfers its ownership to the state.","Rules can apply while ownership remains private."),
 F("public provision","a public body supplying or funding a service","A public body provides road maintenance.","Public provision means no resources were needed.","Public services still use scarce labour, materials and funds."),
 F("policy trade-off","a cost or competing goal involved in a policy choice","Funds devoted to a road cannot simultaneously fund every alternative project.","A policy with benefits has no opportunity cost.","Benefits do not remove the alternatives forgone when resources are used.")],prerequisites=[11,13])

